00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
KASLIN FIELDS: Kubernetes
is an open-source container

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.340
orchestration tool, which
has gained immense popularity

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.290
over the last several years.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.690
As more companies began
to adopt Kubernetes,

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.000
there came to be a demand
for Kubernetes to be

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.640
provided as a managed service.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.320
That's where Google
Kubernetes Engine comes in.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.080
I'm Kaslin Fields,
and I'm a developer

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.670
advocate here at
Google Cloud, where

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:24.210
I focus on Google Kubernetes
Engine, or GKE, as well

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.710
as open-source Kubernetes.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:29.280
And today, we're going to take
a look at some GKE essentials

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.020
around the different
types of cluster

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.590
modes available through GKE.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.957
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.970
Kubernetes is a platform
for running applications

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:45.250
by using the Kubernetes
software to create

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.890
a cluster of
machines, allowing you

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.050
to run your applications
while abstracting

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.840
the hardware that the
applications are running on.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.030
When running open-source
Kubernetes clusters

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:58.120
from scratch, there are a number
of components of the cluster

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.000
itself which you will
need to understand.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.030
These include the control
plane, which itself is made up

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.760
of several
components; as well as

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.750
provisioning and management of
the underlying compute nodes;

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.510
security and networking
configuration of the nodes,

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:15.550
as well as the
cluster as a whole;

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.580
patching and upgrades of
both the cluster software

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.460
and the compute nodes
that make up the cluster;

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.980
monitoring or observability
components, as well

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.710
as management tools, such
as policy-configuration

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:30.730
capabilities; scaling tools,
such as pod-level and cluster

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:35.560
or node-level autoscalers; and
availability and reliability

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.750
capabilities and tools.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:40.930
Some of these components are
built into Kubernetes itself

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:43.270
and some are essential
capabilities which

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.670
may require additional tooling.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:47.560
When running Kubernetes
from scratch,

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.720
you will need to develop
a level of proficiency

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.600
across all of these
categories and be prepared

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.550
for the maintenance,
configuration,

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:57.490
and exploration running a
do-it-yourself cluster will

00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:58.810
require.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.240
Google Kubernetes
Engine, or GKE,

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.660
is a managed Kubernetes
service from Google Cloud.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.600
It allows you to spin up a
Kubernetes cluster quickly,

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.810
with many of the more complex
components of the system being

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:13.270
deployed and managed by Google.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:16.540
This way, you can focus more on
running the applications that

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.510
matter to you on Kubernetes,
rather than spending

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:22.300
as much time understanding
Kubernetes itself.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.480
There are two modes
of operation in GKE.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.020
Each is designed to allow users
different levels of involvement

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.240
with Kubernetes itself.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:35.170
Both Autopilot mode and
Standard mode GKE clusters

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.380
take some of the management
overhead of Kubernetes clusters

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:39.700
off your hands.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:43.000
Standard mode offers a
higher degree of control.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.480
The Kubernetes control plane
and some other considerations

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.970
are handled behind
the scenes by Google,

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:51.430
but you will still need
to manage the nodes

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.070
of the Kubernetes cluster.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:57.430
GKE Autopilot mode provides an
even more hands-off experience.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:00.790
With smart defaults and even
less management overhead,

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:04.990
GKE Autopilot lets you have
more time and resources to spend

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.700
on your own applications.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.190
If you want minimal
cluster-management overhead

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:11.440
in order to focus more of
your time and resources

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.270
on the applications
you need to run,

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:15.670
you may prefer GKE
Autopilot mode.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.700
Autopilot mode GKE clusters
provision infrastructure

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.030
resources as needed for
Kubernetes workloads.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:25.930
This means that you pay
per-pod or per-workload, rather

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:27.940
than per-node.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.560
The nodes of the
Kubernetes cluster

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.120
are automatically
provisioned and managed,

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.390
which means that there
should be no need

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:37.000
to dedicate time and
resources to node management

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.080
and upgrades.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.050
Any infrastructure autoscaling
will be handled automatically

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.190
by the Autopilot mode cluster.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.430
However, you'll need to
configure any desired

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:48.970
autoscaling at the
workload or pod level.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.310
If you've been using
Kubernetes for some time

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:54.370
and have developed skills and
processes that require greater

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.680
control and insight
into the cluster,

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:59.530
you may prefer Standard mode.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:02.500
In Standard mode, the control
plane and some aspects

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.200
of monitoring and management
are handled for you,

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.080
but the user still must manage
the nodes of the Kubernetes

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:09.740
cluster themselves.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:11.320
This means that,
in Standard mode,

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:15.340
you'll pay per-node for the
instance types that you select.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.110
Standard mode
clusters will require

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:20.200
you to dedicate some time and
resources to node management

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.490
and upgrades.

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:24.370
For securing and maintaining
your GKE Standard mode

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:27.640
cluster's worker nodes, be
sure to familiarize yourself

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.500
with the "GKE
Hardening Guide," which

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:32.080
is available in the Google
Cloud documentation.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.180
All four dimensions
of autoscaling

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.550
are available to
standard mode clusters,

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:39.370
but you'll have to manually
configure any desired

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:42.220
autoscalers at both the
workload and infrastructure

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.620
levels for each cluster.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.830
As a managed Kubernetes service,
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:49.900
provides users
with an easier path

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.860
to getting started running their
applications with Kubernetes.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.410
Between Standard mode
and Autopilot mode,

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.800
GKE provides different levels
of control and flexibility,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.960
depending on how
hands-on teams want

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:05.440
to be with managing their
Kubernetes clusters.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:07.730
With the concepts
discussed in this video,

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.280
we're confident
you'll be on your way

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.890
to building a GKE cluster
that suits your needs.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:15.550
You can get started with GKE by
visiting Google Cloud Console

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:17.650
and checking out
the links below.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.470
And stay tuned for our next
videos, where we dive deeper

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:21.460
into GKE topics.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:24.810
